  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 210 PM EST Mon Nov 23 2026\n\nCurrent visible satellite imagery shows decreasing cloud cover \nacross Superior and portions of the U.P. as drier mid- and low-level \nair moves into the Great Lakes Region. Radar imagery indicates that \nsnowbands have shifted eastward into Alger County, which aligns with \nthe 1000-850mb convergence maxima location predicted by the HRRR and \nRAP. Low-level convergence is expected to shift eastward through \nthis evening, resulting the best chances for additional \naccumulations over the eastern U.P. Current NBM guidance indicates a \nlow chance (30%) of accumulations of at least 0.5 inches for the \neasternmost portion of the U.P.\n\nWinds decrease in speed and shift westerly tonight as an area of \nsurface high pressure currently centered over the Northern Plains \nstrengthens and progresses eastward over the Great Lakes along with \na shortwave 500mb ridge. Cloud cover over the U.P. is expected to \ncontinue decreasing during the overnight hours, which will allow \ntemperatures to fall into the teens (5-7 degrees below average) \nthroughout the interior of the forecast area. Tuesday, westerly \nwinds continue to reduce as the area of high pressure progresses \nover the region. Increasing moisture at the upper levels is expected \nto result in high-level cloud cover while surface RHs fall to the \n40s and 50s. Below-average lows are expected again Tuesday night as \ncalm to light westerly winds continue.\n\nLooking towards the holiday weekend, the center of the surface high \npressure will progress eastward over New England on Wednesday as an \nAtlantic Low moves northward into the Carolinas. As the center of \nthe area of high pressure exits the Great Lakes region, light winds \nare expected to shift southerly, resulting in slightly warmer \ntemperatures heading into Thursday. The Atlantic Low is expected to \nmake its way northward over Virgina by midday Thursday, causing \nwinds to shift easterly, then northerly. Current NBM guidance mostly \nkeeps precipitation chances out of the forecast for Thanksgiving, \nwith a 10-30% chance of precipitation along southern Menominee and \nDelta Counties Thursday afternoon. More widespread chances between \n10-40% (highest in the east) of 24-hr precipitation totals over a \ntenth of an inch are indicated by the NBM for Friday night. The \nAtlantic Low is expected to progress up the East Coast and into \neastern Canada, joined by a deepening low in Manitoba overnight \nFriday. Low but nonzero chances for precipitation around a tenth of \nan inch remain in the forecast through the weekend as the Manitoba \nlow crosses to our north. Additional lake effect precipitation may \ntrail the more widespread precipitation chances. Weekend low \ntemperatures are expected to be below freezing, with highs in the \n40s, so, while some precipitation could fall as snow overnight, \nsignificant accumulation is unlikely.",
